---
title: '261006 : questions fermées et ouvertes'
tags:
  - fr
---

Q: In the A1 bilan's yes/no exercises, what response does a `question fermée` request?
A: {{`Oui`}} or {{`non`}}, optionally followed by an explanation.
N: [[fr/lessons/261006#quelle-réponse-attend-on-|Question types]]. Closed questions can also offer a limited choice in other contexts.

---

Q: What does a `question ouverte` request in this lesson?
A: Information such as a date, time, place, price, method, or reason.
N: An open question can have a short answer, such as `À dix heures`. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

Q: Classify `Est-ce que les inscriptions commencent le premier juin ?` as fermée or ouverte.
A: {{`Fermée.`}} It asks for confirmation with yes or no.
N: The proposed start date can be confirmed or denied. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

Q: Classify `Avez-vous des cours le mardi soir ?` as fermée or ouverte.
A: {{`Fermée.`}} It can be answered with yes or no.
N: Inversion joins `avez` and `vous` with a hyphen. [[fr/lessons/261006#les-formes-des-questions-fermées|Closed forms]]

---

Q: Classify `Le centre offre-tu des cours de yoga ?` as fermée or ouverte.
A: {{`Fermée.`}} It asks whether the centre offers yoga classes.
N: Québec spoken interrogative `-tu` stays the same with a named third-person subject.

---

Q: Classify `Vous offrez des cours de yoga prénatal ?` as fermée or ouverte.
A: {{`Fermée.`}} Rising intonation asks for a yes/no answer.
N: Subject–verb order remains unchanged. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

Q: Classify `Comment est-ce qu'on peut s'inscrire ?` as fermée or ouverte.
A: {{`Ouverte.`}} It asks how to register.
N: `Comment` requests a method. `Est-ce que` can occur inside an open question.

---

Q: Classify `À quelle heure commence le cours ?` as fermée or ouverte.
A: {{`Ouverte.`}} It asks for a time.
N: The answer can be brief, such as `À dix heures`. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

Q: Classify `Quels moyens de paiement sont acceptés ?` as fermée or ouverte.
A: {{`Ouverte.`}} It asks for the accepted payment methods.
N: `Quels` agrees with masculine plural `moyens`. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

Q: Classify `Qu'est-ce que comprend l'offre découverte ?` as fermée or ouverte.
A: {{`Ouverte.`}} It asks what the offer includes.
N: The advertisement's offer includes three classes. The recall target is the question type.

---

Q: Change `Est-ce que les inscriptions commencent le premier juin ?` into an open question asking the start date, with `Quand` before `commencent`.
A: {{`Quand commencent les inscriptions ?`}}
N: The open version requests the date instead of proposing one. [[fr/lessons/261006#demander-une-confirmation-ou-une-information|Paired questions]]

---

Q: Change `Est-ce qu'un cours d'essai coute dix dollars ?` into an open question asking its price.
A: {{`Combien coute un cours d'essai ?`}}
N: `Combien` asks for the price here. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

Q: Change `Est-ce qu'on peut s'inscrire par téléphone ?` into an open question asking the registration method, keeping `peut-on`.
A: {{`Comment peut-on s'inscrire ?`}}
N: `Comment` requests the method. `Peut-on` is inversion with `on`.

---

Q: Change `Est-ce que vous acceptez les chèques ?` into an open question asking all accepted methods, starting with `Quels moyens de paiement`.
A: {{`Quels moyens de paiement acceptez-vous ?`}}
N: The verb `acceptez-vous` keeps inversion. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

Q: Ask which day a person's competition in Lanzarote takes place, using `Quel jour a lieu…` and `ta compétition`.
A: {{`Quel jour a lieu ta compétition à Lanzarote ?`}}
N: Practice model. No real competition date is assumed. [[fr/lessons/261006#autres-situations-à-pratiquer|Other situations]]

---

Q: Turn `C'est une montre.` into a closed question using `Est-ce que`.
A: {{`Est-ce que c'est une montre ?`}}
N: The question asks whether the object is a watch. The open version is `Qu'est-ce que c'est ?`.

---

C: Pour demander un prix : `[Combien|how much] coute un cours d'essai ?`
N: An open question about price. [[fr/lessons/261006#les-formes-des-questions-ouvertes|Question words]]

---

C: Pour demander le moyen : `[Comment|how] peut-on s'inscrire ?`
N: An open question about the registration method.

---

C: Pour demander la date : `[Quand|when] commencent les inscriptions ?`
N: `Commencent` agrees with plural `les inscriptions`.

---

C: Pour demander le lieu : `[Où|where] est le centre de yoga ?`
N: An open question about location. The accent distinguishes `où` from `ou`, meaning or.

---

C: Accord masculin singulier : `[Quel] est le prix du cours ?`
N: `Quel` agrees with masculine singular `prix`.

---

C: Accord féminin singulier : `[Quelle] est la date de début ?`
N: `Quelle` agrees with feminine singular `date`.

---

C: Accord masculin pluriel : `[Quels] moyens de paiement acceptez-vous ?`
N: `Quels` agrees with masculine plural `moyens`.

---

C: Accord féminin pluriel : `[Quelles] sont les dates de la session ?`
N: `Quelles` agrees with feminine plural `dates`. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

Q: In the neighbourhood vocabulary, what is a shop called `un nettoyeur` in English?
A: A dry cleaner's.
N: The class note links `nettoyeurs de quartier` with `nettoyage à sec`. Here `nettoyeur` refers to the business. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-10-par-ici-exercice|Neighbourhood vocabulary]]

---

Q: Say “dry cleaning” in French.
A: {{`Le nettoyage à sec.`}}
N: The cleaning service named in the class notes. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-10-par-ici-exercice|Neighbourhood vocabulary]]

---

Q: Say “a jewellery shop” in French using the word beginning with `bij-`.
A: {{`Une bijouterie.`}}
N: The note also mentions `la joaillerie`, jewellery-making or fine jewellery; the terms have overlapping uses. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

Q: Say “an optometrist” in French, using a masculine indefinite article.
A: {{`Un optométriste.`}}
N: A professional in the neighbourhood vocabulary. The definite form is `l'optométriste`. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

Q: What French place name fits the class definition “un endroit où on retrouve plusieurs magasins”?
A: {{`Un centre commercial.`}}
N: Model answer to the definition in the episode 10 class notes. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

Q: Name “a place where people have their hair cut” in French, using `salon`.
A: {{`Un salon de coiffure.`}}
N: Model answer to the class definition about haircuts. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-10-par-ici-exercice|Neighbourhood vocabulary]]

---

Q: Name the place where you `faites le plein de gaz`, using the French term `station`.
A: {{`Une station-service.`}}
N: `Faire le plein` means to fill the fuel tank. `Gaz` is the familiar Québec wording in the class notes. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

C: Dans le centre commercial, il y a beaucoup [de|preposition] magasins.
N: `Beaucoup de` + noun: many shops. With `plusieurs`, there is no `de`: `plusieurs magasins`. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

Q: What does the French verb `cueillir` mean in the vocabulary note?
A: To pick or gather, for example fruit or flowers.
N: The note pairs the verb with its noun `cueillette`. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-10-par-ici-exercice|Class vocabulary]]

---

Q: Give the French noun for picking or gathering that corresponds to `cueillir`.
A: {{`La cueillette.`}}
N: The verb-noun pair recorded in class: `cueillir → cueillette`. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

Q: What does `un renseignement` mean in English?
A: A piece of information.
N: `Des renseignements` means information or details. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-10-par-ici-exercice|Class vocabulary]]

---

Q: Say “to find out / to ask for information” using the reflexive verb from the class note.
A: {{`Se renseigner.`}}
N: The person seeks information for themselves. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

Q: What does `renseigner quelqu'un` mean?
A: To give someone information.
N: This is the direction recorded in the class note: `renseigner qqn = donner des informations à qqn`. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

Q: Give the Québec cash-payment expression equivalent to `payer en liquide` in the class note.
A: {{`Payer en argent comptant.`}}
N: `Payer comptant` is also possible. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-10-par-ici-exercice|Class vocabulary]]

---

Q: Rephrase `Que faites-vous ?` using `Qu'est-ce que` and subject–verb order.
A: {{`Qu'est-ce que vous faites ?`}}
N: Equivalent open questions from the episode 13 class notes. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-13|Episode 13 notes]]

---

C: Pour demander ce qui ne va pas : `Qu'est-[ce] qui ne va pas ?`
N: Corrected question model. The raw class note has `Qu'est qui ne va pas ?`; the missing `ce` is needed. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-13|Episode 13 notes]]

---

Q: Ask a sick friend “What's wrong?” using `tu` and the verb `avoir`.
A: {{`Qu'est-ce que tu as ?`}}
N: Literally “What do you have?” In this context, it asks about the person's problem or symptoms. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-13|Episode 13 notes]]

---

Q: Ask “What's the matter?” using the question from the class notes that ends in `il y a`.
A: {{`Qu'est-ce qu'il y a ?`}}
N: An open question about what is happening. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-13|Episode 13 notes]]

---

Q: Say “Things aren't going well at all” using the class expression `ça` and `du tout`.
A: {{`Ça ne va pas du tout.`}}
N: Stronger than saying things are not going very well. `Pas du tout` means not at all. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-13|Episode 13 notes]]

---

Q: Give the two-word sympathy expression meaning “Poor you!” from the class notes.
A: {{`Pauvre toi !`}}
N: Used to express sympathy to the sick person. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-13|Episode 13 notes]]

---

Q: In the sick-person exchange, what does `C'est pas drôle, ça !` convey?
A: Sympathy: “That's no fun” or “That isn't pleasant.”
N: Familiar speech omits `ne`; formal written wording is `Ce n'est pas drôle`. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-13|Episode 13 notes]]

---

Q: Give the familiar Québec reaction “That's a shame” using `plate`.
A: {{`C'est plate !`}}
N: In the illness exchange it expresses disappointment or sympathy; in other contexts `plate` can mean boring. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-13|Episode 13 notes]]

---

C: Futur proche : `Elle [va|aller, présent] aller au lit.`
N: The conjugated `va` introduces the infinitive `aller`. She is going to go to bed. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-13|Episode 13 notes]]

---

Q: What does `aller se reposer` mean in the class note?
A: To go and rest.
N: The note pairs going to bed with going to rest in the illness context. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-13|Episode 13 notes]]

---

Q: Say “to retire” using the phrase in the class notes beginning with `partir`.
A: {{`Partir à la retraite.`}}
N: The note explains this as `arrêter de travailler` in the retirement context. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-13|Episode 13 notes]]

---

Q: What does `attraper un rhume` mean?
A: To catch a cold.
N: The class sentence is `Je pense que j'ai attrapé un bon gros rhume.` `J'ai attrapé` describes catching it in the past. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-13|Episode 13 notes]]

---

C: Pour le nom “illness” : `On peut attraper une [maladie].`
N: Corrected vocabulary: `une maladie` is an illness. `Une malade` refers to a sick woman or girl, so it does not name the illness intended in the class note. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-13|Episode 13 notes]]

---

Q: Give “the flu” in French with its definite article.
A: {{`La grippe.`}}
N: `Grippe` is feminine, as in the class note `une grippe`. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-13|Episode 13 notes]]

---

Q: Which definite article does the Québec French class note use before `COVID`?
A: {{`La COVID.`}}
N: The feminine article is the Québec usage recorded in the note. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-13|Episode 13 notes]]

---

Q: Change `Soigne-toi bien !` to address someone as `vous`.
A: {{`Soignez-vous bien !`}}
N: Keep the reflexive pronoun after the imperative and join it with a hyphen. Practice from the class expression. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-13|Episode 13 notes]]

---

Q: Turn `Tu te soignes bien.` into advice at the imperative, addressing one friend as `tu`.
A: {{`Soigne-toi bien !`}}
N: This regular `-er` imperative drops the final `s`; `te` becomes `toi` after the verb. [[fr/lessons/261006#episode-13|Episode 13 notes]]

---

Q: Answer `Est-ce que les cours d'essai sont gratuits ?` using the yoga advertisement in the lesson.
A: {{`Non, un cours d'essai coute dix dollars.`}}
N: The offer is for new clients. This answers the later class question without confusing the 10-dollar trial with the 17-dollar discovery offer. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

C: Pour demander le prix de trois mois : `Combien coute un abonnement [de] trois mois ?`
N: Complete question model for the raw fragment `un abonnement des trois`. The duration is expressed as `de trois mois`. [[fr/lessons/261006#questions-notées-en-cours|Class questions]]

---

Q: Ask informally when someone's birthday is, using `C'est quand` and `ton anniversaire`.
A: {{`C'est quand, ton anniversaire ?`}}
N: Asks for the date. No personal birthday is assumed. [[fr/lessons/261006#autres-situations-à-pratiquer|Other situations]]

---

Q: Ask which weekday someone's birthday falls on this year, starting with `Ton anniversaire tombe`.
A: {{`Ton anniversaire tombe quel jour cette année ?`}}
N: `Cette année` fixes the year for the weekday question. This is distinct from asking the birthday's date. [[fr/lessons/261006]]

---

Q: Ask what someone uses to tell the time, using `tu` and `savoir l'heure`.
A: {{`Qu'est-ce que tu utilises pour savoir l'heure ?`}}
N: Open question about the object used, for example a watch or a cellulaire. [[fr/lessons/261006#autres-situations-à-pratiquer|Other situations]]

---

Q: In the friendship box, what does `x` mean at the end of an affectionate message?
A: {{`Un bisou.`}} A kiss.
N: The supplied episode 13 image describes this North American message convention. [[fr/lessons/261006#lamitié-et-laffection--ailleurs-autrement-|Friendship and affection]]

---

Q: In the friendship box, what does `o` mean at the end of an affectionate message?
A: {{`Un câlin.`}} A hug.
N: `XOXO` combines kisses and hugs. [[fr/lessons/261006#lamitié-et-laffection--ailleurs-autrement-|Friendship and affection]]

---

Q: Say “a hug” in French, with its indefinite article.
A: {{`Un câlin.`}}
N: Masculine; retain the circumflex in `câlin`. [[fr/lessons/261006#lamitié-et-laffection--ailleurs-autrement-|Vocabulary]]

---

Q: What does `faire la bise` mean in the greeting notes?
A: To greet someone with cheek kisses.
N: The image describes kisses on both cheeks between people who are close to each other in Québec. [[fr/lessons/261006#lamitié-et-laffection--ailleurs-autrement-|Greetings]]

---

Q: What does `se saluer` mean when two people meet?
A: To greet each other.
N: The image uses `pour se saluer` to explain the purpose of the greeting. [[fr/lessons/261006#lamitié-et-laffection--ailleurs-autrement-|Vocabulary]]

---

Q: Which people does the Québec greeting description identify as kissing on both cheeks?
A: {{`Des personnes proches.`}} People who are close to each other.
N: Preserve this relationship qualifier from the image; the note does not prescribe a greeting for every encounter. [[fr/lessons/261006#lamitié-et-laffection--ailleurs-autrement-|Source context]]

---

Q: What does `embrasser quelqu'un` mean in these affection notes?
A: To kiss someone.
N: Example: {{`J'embrasse mon amie.`}} For a hug, the notes use `faire un câlin`. [[fr/lessons/261006#lamitié-et-laffection--ailleurs-autrement-|Embrasser]]

---

Q: What does `Elles s'embrassent` mean in the reciprocal greeting context?
A: They kiss each other.
N: `Se` becomes `s'` before the vowel. For “They hug each other”: {{`Elles se font un câlin.`}} [[fr/lessons/261006#lamitié-et-laffection--ailleurs-autrement-|Grammar]]

---

Q: Say “We kiss each other” using the reciprocal verb `s'embrasser` in the present tense.
A: {{`Nous nous embrassons.`}}
N: The first `nous` is the subject; the second is the reciprocal pronoun. [[fr/lessons/261006#lamitié-et-laffection--ailleurs-autrement-|Grammar]]
